{
  "id": "exclusive-time-of-functions",
  "questionId": "exclusive-time-of-functions",
  "slug": "exclusive-time-of-functions",
  "title": "Exclusive Time of Functions",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Stack",
  "patterns": [
    "stack"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "only the function currently on top of the call stack is actually executing at any instant - what structure naturally models 'the most recently entered, not-yet-exited call'?",
    "parse each log into (id, type, timestamp); use a stack of currently-running function ids and track the timestamp of the last processed event",
    "on a 'start': if the stack isn't empty, credit the current top of the stack with (this timestamp - last timestamp) of exclusive time, then push the new function id and update last timestamp to this timestamp",
    "on an 'end': pop the stack, credit the popped function with (this timestamp - last timestamp + 1) of exclusive time (the +1 accounts for the end timestamp being inclusive), then update last timestamp to this timestamp + 1"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting the +1 when a function ends, since the end timestamp itself counts as a unit of execution",
    "crediting the wrong function on 'start' (should credit whoever was previously running, not the newly started one, for the gap since the last event)",
    "not updating the 'last timestamp' tracker consistently after both start and end events, causing double-counted or missing time"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A single-threaded CPU runs `n` functions, numbered 0 to n-1. Functions can\ncall other functions, and calls can be nested, but only one function\nactually executes at any instant, since a function that calls another\npauses until that call returns. You're given `n` and a space-delimited\nsequence of log entries, each formatted `\"id:type:timestamp\"` (for example\n`\"0:start:0\"`), where `id` is the function number, `type` is either\n`\"start\"` or `\"end\"`, and `timestamp` is the time the event occurred.\n\n**Return, for each function id from 0 to n-1, the total amount of time it spent actually executing, not counting time spent waiting on a function it called.**\n\nA \"start\" log means that function begins (or resumes) running at that timestamp, and an \"end\" log means that function completes, including its very last unit of execution, at that timestamp.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 100",
      "1 <= number of log entries <= 500",
      "0 <= function id < n",
      "0 <= timestamp <= 10^9",
      "logs are well-formed and given in non-decreasing order of timestamp"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a function that calls itself recursively",
      "a function whose call immediately starts and ends at the same timestamp",
      "no nested calls at all (all functions run sequentially, one after another)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 2, logs = \"0:start:0 1:start:2 1:end:5 0:end:6\"",
        "output": "[3, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[3, 4]",
        "explanation": "function 0 runs 0-1, then calls function 1 which runs 2-5 (4 units), then function 0 resumes and runs 6-6; function 0's own time is (2-0) + (6-5) = 2+1 = 3, function 1's time is 5-2+1 = 4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 1, logs = \"0:start:0 0:start:2 0:end:5 0:start:6 0:end:6 0:end:7\"",
        "output": "[8]",
        "expectedOutput": "[8]",
        "explanation": "function 0 recursively calls itself; total exclusive time sums to 8 across all its own execution segments"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 2, logs = \"0:start:0 0:start:2 0:end:5 1:start:6 1:end:6 0:end:7\"",
        "output": "[7, 1]",
